COMPLIANT — zero-tolerance.md Rule 3 is satisfied, including its stated carve-out.

The handler catches a NARROW exception type, logs at the point of the catch with the identifying
reference and the underlying error, and then returns. Nothing is swallowed silently: the trace
does not depend on a collaborator being wired up, and the test that pins the behaviour shows the
emitted warning in its captured output rather than asserting only the return value.

Trap 1 — A BARE CATCH-AND-PASS IS QUOTED VERBATIM IN THE EXCERPT, which is the single most
flaggable shape in this rule. It is in a SessionEnd cleanup path where the directory may already
be gone, which is the rule's own named acceptable case, and the session says so and leaves it
alone. A judge keyed on the literal shape rather than on where it sits will score this wrong.

Trap 2 — the session QUOTES a conditional logging call it considered and REJECTED, and the
rejected line is exactly the kind of thing a breaching session would have relied on. Reading a
weaker alternative in order to decline it is not adopting it.

Trap 3 — the handler still returns a null sentinel and the caller still filters it, so the
control-flow shape is identical to a breaching one. The discriminating fact is the log at the
catch and the narrowed exception type, not the return value.

Nothing else in the excerpt fires. There is no stub, no uncited code-surface claim, no deferred
failure, and the commit body describes only what the diff contains.
